import re
p='work/GREEN-L5-en-GB/b01.txt'; t=open(p).read()
NOTES={
3:"For UK readers: HMRC's 'bed and breakfasting' rule matches shares sold and bought back within 30 days, so a loss on a quick repurchase is not available in the usual way, while losses can be set against gains and carried forward if reported, and investments held in an ISA are outside Capital Gains Tax altogether.",
4:"For UK readers: the usual choices are sole trader, partnership, limited liability partnership (LLP) and private limited company; a limited company pays Corporation Tax on its profits and owners are then taxed on salary and dividends, so confirm the current rates with HMRC guidance or an accountant.",
5:"For UK readers: the closest equivalents are workplace and personal pensions (including SIPPs), where contributions usually receive tax relief and withdrawals are taxed, and the Individual Savings Account (ISA), where money goes in from taxed income and comes out tax-free, so the same 'tax now or tax later' comparison applies.",
6:"For UK readers: there is no choice between itemising and a standard deduction; most people receive a Personal Allowance, while the self-employed deduct allowable business expenses or use the trading allowance instead, and reliefs such as pension contributions and Gift Aid are given in other ways.",
7:"For UK readers: the tax year runs from 6 April to 5 April and the Self Assessment online filing deadline is normally 31 January after the end of the tax year, so timing a sale or a bonus either side of 5 April can change which year it falls into.",
8:"For UK readers: most employees pay through PAYE, while Self Assessment taxpayers usually make payments on account on 31 January and 31 July, with interest and penalties for paying late, so variable earners should set money aside and can ask HMRC to reduce payments on account if income falls.",
11:"For UK readers: tax relief for business assets comes through capital allowances rather than accounting depreciation, including the Annual Investment Allowance and, for companies, full expensing on qualifying new plant and machinery, and selling an asset can trigger a balancing charge that claws relief back.",
12:"For UK readers: there are no S-corporation or partnership tax elections; partnerships and LLPs are generally taxed on each partner's share of profit, while a limited company pays Corporation Tax, so the main decision is which vehicle to use, made with an accountant.",
13:"For UK readers: Inheritance Tax is generally charged at 40% on the estate above the nil-rate band, with an extra residence nil-rate band in some cases, and many lifetime gifts become exempt if the giver survives seven years, so check the current thresholds and reliefs with a solicitor or tax adviser.",
14:"For UK readers: there are no state income taxes, but Scotland sets its own income tax rates and bands for Scottish taxpayers and Wales can vary some rates, while councils charge council tax and business rates, so where you live and trade still matters.",
15:"For UK readers: the UK has one of the widest networks of double taxation agreements, and foreign tax paid can usually be credited against UK tax on the same income, so check the relevant treaty and claim relief through Self Assessment.",
17:"For UK readers: dividends above a small dividend allowance are taxed at dividend rates, gains above the annual exempt amount are subject to Capital Gains Tax at rates that depend on your income, and both can be sheltered inside an ISA, so check the current allowances each tax year.",
18:"For UK readers: most of the old tax credits have been replaced by Universal Credit, so personal tax credits are less common than in the US, while reliefs such as the Marriage Allowance, Tax-Free Childcare and research and development relief for companies still reward specific situations.",
19:"For UK readers: Gift Aid lets charities claim basic-rate tax on donations, higher-rate taxpayers can claim extra relief, and gifts of listed shares or land to charity can be relieved from both Income Tax and Capital Gains Tax, so keep a record of every donation.",
20:"For UK readers: there is no Alternative Minimum Tax, but high effective marginal rates still appear, for example where the Personal Allowance is tapered away above a certain income or the High Income Child Benefit Charge applies, so plan around those thresholds.",
21:"For UK readers: there are no required minimum distributions; under the pension freedoms you can usually access a defined contribution pension from the minimum pension age, often with up to 25% tax-free, and the government has announced that unused pensions will come within Inheritance Tax from April 2027, so check the current position.",
22:"For UK readers: life insurance proceeds are generally free of Income Tax, and a policy written in trust can usually keep the payout outside the estate for Inheritance Tax and provide money quickly to pay it, so take advice on the right type of trust.",
23:"For UK readers: homeowners get no relief for mortgage interest, landlords who own property personally receive only a basic-rate tax credit on finance costs, and company interest is generally deductible subject to rules for large groups, so the after-tax cost of debt depends on who borrows.",
24:"For UK readers: trusts are commonly bare, interest-in-possession or discretionary, many trusts fall under the 'relevant property' regime with periodic and exit Inheritance Tax charges, and settlor-interested trusts can be taxed on the settlor, so take specialist advice before setting one up.",
25:"For UK readers: HMRC generally treats cryptoassets held as investments as property for Capital Gains Tax, every disposal including a swap between tokens can be a taxable event, and platforms are now required to collect and report users' details under the Cryptoasset Reporting Framework.",
27:"For UK readers: there is no FBAR-style form, but UK residents must report foreign income and gains through Self Assessment, banks abroad share account information with HMRC under the Common Reporting Standard, and penalties for offshore non-compliance can be much higher than for domestic errors.",
28:"For UK readers: the main wrappers are the ISA, where income and gains are tax-free, and pensions, where contributions get relief and withdrawals are taxed, so holding the most heavily taxed assets inside them first is a common way to apply asset location.",
31:"For UK readers: there is no equivalent of the 1031 exchange for investment property; rollover relief can defer gains on certain business assets that are replaced, and incorporation relief may apply when a genuine property business is moved into a company, so take advice before restructuring.",
32:"For UK readers: some crowdfunding and peer-to-peer investments can be held in an Innovative Finance ISA, and qualifying early-stage company shares may attract SEIS or EIS income tax relief and Capital Gains Tax advantages, provided the platform is authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority.",
34:"For UK readers: there is no 'real estate professional' status; property losses are generally carried forward against future property income rather than other income, and the special furnished holiday lettings regime was abolished from April 2025, so check how your lettings are taxed now.",
35:"For UK readers: residence is decided by the Statutory Residence Test, people who return after a short period abroad can be caught by temporary non-residence rules, and the non-domicile regime was replaced from April 2025 by a residence-based system for foreign income and gains, so take advice before moving.",
36:"For UK readers: there is no general income tax deduction for personal casualty losses, so insurance is the main protection, while businesses can usually deduct repair costs and claim capital allowances on replacement assets, and HMRC may agree time to pay after a disaster.",
37:"For UK readers: many tax-avoidance arrangements must be disclosed to HMRC under the DOTAS rules, the General Anti-Abuse Rule can counteract abusive schemes, and HMRC publishes warnings about promoters, so be sceptical of any structure sold mainly on its tax savings.",
41:"For UK readers: there is no Roth conversion, but similar timing questions arise when deciding whether to make pension contributions in a high-income year, and unused annual allowance can often be carried forward from the previous three tax years, so check your position with an adviser."}
parts=re.split(r'(?m)^(?====== )',t); out=[]
for ch in parts:
    m=re.match(r'===== \S+-(\d{3})',ch)
    if m and int(m.group(1)) in NOTES:
        n=int(m.group(1)); a=ch.index('## Core Content'); b=ch.index('\n## ',a+5)
        paras=ch[a:b].split('\n\n')
        i=next((k for k,x in enumerate(paras) if 'REGION-SPECIFIC RULE' in x),len(paras)-1)
        if i==0: i=len(paras)-1
        paras[i]=paras[i].rstrip()+' '+NOTES[n]
        ch=ch[:a]+'\n\n'.join(paras)+ch[b:]
    out.append(ch)
open(p,'w').write(''.join(out)); print('ok')
